# OpenBeach @VERSION@ (@PLATFORM@): instructions for an AI agent

The user downloaded this archive and wants to play Beach Life (2002) with OpenBeach. Your job: import their own copy of
the game once, then start the game. Talk to the user in their language; `README.<language>.txt` has the same steps for
people, one file per language. Source and documentation: https://github.com/h4570/openbeach

## Layout
```
README.<language>.txt  for the user
AGENTS.md           this file
game/
[win]  OpenBeach.exe     the game (also started by Play.cmd and Demo.cmd)
[win]  obl-convert.exe   the converter that reads the user's copy (started by Import.cmd)
[win]  Import.cmd        scripts\import.ps1: the import with prompts
[linux]  OpenBeach         the game (also started by play.sh and demo.sh)
[linux]  obl-convert       the converter that reads the user's copy (started by import.sh)
[linux]  import.sh         the import with prompts
  userdata/         the game directory: converted pack, settings, saves, log (empty until the import)
  l0/               the game directory of the demo (the project's own L0 pack, no original data)
```
[win]
[win]The installer (`openbeach-@VERSION@-windows-x64-installer.exe`) lays out the same tree in the folder the user chose
[win](by default `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\OpenBeach`) and imports an `.iso` with `game\scripts\import.ps1`. If
[win]`game\userdata\packs\base\pack.json` exists, the copy is imported already: go to step 4.

## Rules
- **Never modify the user's copy.** The converter only reads it; never write, move or delete anything in it.
- **Never download the game or an .iso for the user.** OpenBeach needs the user's own legal copy. If they have none, say
  so and offer the demo.
- Ask before deleting `game/userdata/`: it holds the user's saves (`SaveGames/`) and settings.
- Do not move the binaries out of `game/`: the scripts and the game find each other by that folder.

## 1. Check the system
[win]- Windows 10 or 11, x64. PowerShell 5.1 or newer is part of Windows.
[linux]- x86-64 Linux with glibc 2.35 or newer: `ldd --version`. The game needs an X11 or Wayland session.
[linux]- Run-time libraries: `README.en.txt`, "LIBRARIES" (Debian/Ubuntu package list). Without them SDL falls
[linux]  back (no OpenGL: software rendering, no sound server: silence); a missing X11/Wayland library stops the window.

## 2. Find the user's copy
Ask the user where it is; do not search their whole disk without asking. Any of these works:
- an installed game: a folder with `BeachLife.exe` and `Data/`;
- the CD in a drive, or its contents copied to a folder (InstallShield `data1.hdr`, `data1.cab`, ...);
- a disc image `.iso` of the CD.

Supported copies: the European CD, the French/German CD, the UK/US CD (*Virtual Resort: Spring Break*), the later
English CD, and installations of these (also the digital release over a CD installation). The converter identifies the
copy by hashes and refuses an unknown one with a message, without writing anything of it.

## 3. Import (once, about 15 minutes)
[win]```powershell
[win]powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File game\scripts\import.ps1 -Source "<copy>" [-Language <code>]
[win]```
[win]`<copy>` is the folder, the drive (`E:\`) or the `.iso` file: the script mounts an image with `Mount-DiskImage` and
[win]unmounts it afterwards. If mounting fails, ask the user to double-click the `.iso` and use the new drive letter.
[linux]```sh
[linux]game/import.sh "<copy>" [<code>]
[linux]```
[linux]`<copy>` is the folder or the `.iso` file: the script mounts an image with `udisksctl` (no sudo) and unmounts it
[linux]afterwards. If that fails, mount it yourself (`sudo mount -o loop,ro <file.iso> <dir>`, with the user's consent) and
[linux]pass the directory.

The script imports into `game/userdata/packs/base.new/` and replaces `game/userdata/packs/base/` with it only on success
(a failed import keeps the old pack), writes `game/userdata/remaster/` (cursors) and sets `[Language] Language=<index>`
in `game/userdata/BeachLife.ini`, keeping other settings.
[linux]It also adds `~/.local/share/applications/openbeach.desktop` with the OpenBeach icon (the applications menu).
Run it with a long timeout or in the background: it prints one line per step and ends with `Done.` and exit code 0.

**Language.** The pack gets the language of the installation's `BeachLife.ini`. A CD names none: the converter stops with
an error about the language, and the script then asks on standard input. Pass the language up front instead when you
run it without a terminal: `en` (UK/US and later English CD) or one of `fr de it pl es` (European and French/German CD);
the copy must have that table.
[win]`-PreferLanguage <code>` instead takes that language when the copy has it, else one it has, and asks nothing.
The game's index order is `en fr it de es pl` (0 to 5). To switch later to another language the pack has
(`game/userdata/packs/base/text/<code>.json`), change `Language=` in `game/userdata/BeachLife.ini`.

Manual equivalent of the script (the converter alone):
[win]```powershell
[win]game\obl-convert.exe import "<copy>" game\userdata\packs\base [--language <code>]
[win]game\obl-convert.exe resources "<copy>" game\userdata\remaster
[win]```
[linux]```sh
[linux]game/obl-convert import "<copy>" game/userdata/packs/base [--language <code>]
[linux]game/obl-convert resources "<copy>" game/userdata/remaster
[linux]```
Both target directories must exist. Then write the INI as above: without `[Beach Life Options]` and `Version=1` the
game ignores the file and starts in English, which a European copy does not have.

## 4. Start the game
[win]Double-click `game\Play.cmd`, or `game\OpenBeach.exe --game-dir game\userdata`. `game\OpenBeach.exe` without
[win]arguments also uses `game\userdata\`.
[linux]`game/play.sh` (= `game/OpenBeach --game-dir game/userdata`). `game/OpenBeach` without arguments also uses
[linux]`game/userdata/`.

The game opens a window: intro movie, two logo screens, then the main menu. Tell the user it is ready; do not leave it
running for them to find unless they asked you to start it.

Success check without looking at the screen: `game/userdata/openbeach.log` gets `init done` shortly after the start.
A missing or broken pack stops the start with a message naming the file and "Import the game data again": run step 3.

## Demo without the original game
[win]`game\Demo.cmd` (= `OBL_PACK=l0`, `OpenBeach.exe --game-dir game\l0`).
[linux]`game/demo.sh` (= `OBL_PACK=l0 game/OpenBeach --game-dir game/l0`).

## Troubleshooting
- Converter errors are printed with `error:`; an unknown executable or sprite stamp means an unsupported copy.
[win]- SmartScreen ("Windows protected your PC") blocks unsigned files downloaded from the internet: the user clicks
[win]  "More info", "Run anyway", or unblocks the .zip (Properties, Unblock) before unpacking.
- Log: `game/userdata/openbeach.log` (the demo: `game/l0/openbeach.log`). Bugs go to
  https://github.com/h4570/openbeach/issues with that log.
- Saves of the original game: copy them into `game/userdata/SaveGames/`; the game converts them when it starts.
- Updating: unpack the new version and copy the old `game/userdata/` into it. If the new version asks to import the game
  data again, run step 3.
